# Amusements: generators, simulators and diversions that are not quite
# games.  One stanza per program.  logisim (Maths & calculators) and
# textb (Screen toys) have lived here since the sheet was written and stay.

shot    areacode
cap     Five codes asked at once: 212, 415, 314 and 617 answered with the
cap     cities they cover, and 999 is not an area code.
try     areacode 212 415 314 617 999
run     areacode 212 415 314 617 999
wait    4

shot    biory
size    44 100
cap     A biorhythm chart, in German.  It asks for a name (Name Vorname), a
cap     birth date as six digits, day month year (Geburtsdatum), and the first
cap     and last years to chart, two digits each (Gueltigkeitsbereich), writes
cap     the chart to Biory.Lis in the current directory and asks for the next
cap     name; RETURN there ends it.  Each month is three rows -- Koerper
cap     (body), Seele (emotion), Geist (mind) -- with K marking the day a
cap     cycle crosses from high to low or back.  It needs the Fortran
cap     run-time, os9lib, loaded first.
try     biory
run     load /dd/CMDS/os9lib
wait    2
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/BIOA; mkdir -p /dd/tmp/BIOA
wait    2
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/BIOA
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     biory
wait    5
send    Anna Bergmann\r
wait    3
send    140365\r
wait    3
send    88-88\r
wait    8
send    \r
wait    6
run     cat Biory.Lis | head -n 32
wait    5

shot    bio
size    52 80
cap     A biorhythm chart written in BASIC09, so it runs under runb.  It
cap     asks for a date and a birthday, both as day.month.year with the
cap     year in four digits (RETURN at the date prompt takes today), then
cap     whether you want that day's values
cap     (v) or a graph (g), and for a graph how many days.  Each row is a
cap     day; P, E and M are the physical, emotional and mental cycles,
cap     swinging from bad condition on the left to good on the right.
try     runb bio
run     load /h1/CMDS/runb
wait    2
run     clear
wait    1
run     runb bio
wait    3
send    01.06.2025\r
wait    2
send    15.06.1970\r
wait    2
send    g\r
wait    3
send    30\r
wait    6

shot    england
size    40 100
cap     January of the first year, a day a line: sky, temperature,
cap     precipitation, wind, snow on the ground and the day's notes, holidays
cap     among them.  `england 2' does two years.
try     england | head -n 37
os9     england ! head -n 37
run     england | head -n 37
wait    10

shot    florida
size    40 100
cap     The weather program on its Gulf-coast profile: January is warm,
cap     and what falls is rain and the odd storm rather than snow.
try     florida | head -n 37
os9     florida ! head -n 37
run     florida | head -n 37
wait    10

shot    georgia
size    40 100
cap     The weather program on its south-Atlantic profile: a mild, wet
cap     January, cloud and rain on a handful of days and no snow on the
cap     ground.
try     georgia | head -n 37
os9     georgia ! head -n 37
run     georgia | head -n 37
wait    10

shot    minnesota
size    40 100
cap     The weather program on its north-Atlantic profile: January is
cap     cold throughout, and the snow depth in the seventh column grows
cap     with it.
try     minnesota | head -n 37
os9     minnesota ! head -n 37
run     minnesota | head -n 37
wait    10

shot    japan
size    40 100
cap     The weather program on its north-Pacific profile, with the months
cap     of the old Japanese calendar -- Rat, Bull and so on -- and its own
cap     notes: Shinto and Buddhist days, the full and new moons, the
cap     equinox.
try     japan | head -n 37
os9     japan ! head -n 37
run     japan | head -n 37
wait    10

shot    shire
size    40 100
cap     The weather program on the Shire calendar from the appendices of
cap     The Lord of the Rings -- Afteryule is the first month, Solmath the
cap     second -- over the mid-Atlantic climate.
try     shire | head -n 37
os9     shire ! head -n 37
run     shire | head -n 37
wait    10

shot    logisim
size    40 100
cap     A logic simulator.  It reads a circuit written as text -- one gate a
cap     line, named by type, with its input nodes and its output -- and runs
cap     it as a pulse diagram: a row for each node named on the circuit's
cap     `view' line, a column a step, # for high and _ for low, with the step
cap     numbers in the bars above and below.  This is the 16-bit counter that
cap     ships with it in DOC/logisim, counting up from the top row; the other
cap     sample there is a master-slave flip-flop whose input you set at a
cap     prompt.  Esc stops the run.  PORT must name your terminal, because it
cap     reopens the keyboard through that path.
try     PORT=/term logisim DOC/logisim/counter.lsi
os9     setenv PORT /term; logisim DOC/logisim/counter.lsi
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     PORT=/term logisim DOC/logisim/counter.lsi
wait    10
snap
send    \033
wait    2
send    y
wait    3

shot    name
cap     Eight names from `name 8'; the apostrophe counts as a letter, as in
cap     Eb'duna.
try     name 8
run     name 8
wait    4

shot    rndname
cap     The earlier version of `name': the same name-maker with every
cap     letter equally likely, so x, z and q turn up as often as t and n
cap     and the names come out more exotic.
try     rndname 6
run     rndname 6
wait    5

shot    newsgen
size    30 80
cap     A news bulletin made up at random from parts: a top story pieced
cap     together from public figures, deeds, places and reactions, then
cap     the weather.  Different every run.
try     newsgen
run     newsgen
wait    6

shot    pwgen
cap     Five pronounceable passwords from `pwgen 8 5', strung together from
cap     syllables that can be said and so remembered.  Each is seven letters:
cap     the length counts the end of the string, so it gives one fewer letter
cap     than it is asked for.
try     pwgen 8 5
run     pwgen 8 5
wait    20

shot    rpoem
size    30 80
cap     Three verses, each four lines on one pattern -- a thing that should or
cap     may do something, then `But' and `While' -- with the words drawn at
cap     random from its grammar in GAMES/SNOBOL.
try     rpoem 3
run     rpoem 3
wait    8

shot    rstory
size    64 100
cap     One telling, set by tformat under its dated heading: a kind old aunt,
cap     a dog that will not cross a fallen tree, and the chain of water,
cap     farmer, sword, man and knife that at last gets her home.  Every
cap     telling draws its own.  Without tformat it prints the formatting
cap     commands as `.b' and `.c' lines above the text.
try     rstory | tformat
os9     rstory ! tformat
run     rstory | tformat
wait    12

shot    scales
size    30 80
cap     `scales -d -c' deals the diatonic scales and the chords, 195 cards,
cap     into scales.lst; the first seven, each with a tick-box and its
cap     spelling.
try     scales -d -c
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/SCLA > /nil 2>/nil; mkdir -p /dd/tmp/SCLA > /nil 2>/nil
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/SCLA
wait    2
run     clear
wait    1
run     scales -d -c
wait    6
run     cat scales.lst | head -n 16
wait    4

shot    textb
size    32 80
cap     The Mandelbrot set drawn in ASCII, the number of iterations at
cap     each point turned into a character.  It asks for the lower-left
cap     corner, the width of the square to draw and the iteration limit;
cap     -2.3, -2.0, 4.0 and 32 gives the whole set, cardioid and bulb
cap     both, and a smaller range zooms in.
try     textb
run     textb
wait    4
send    -2.3\r
wait    3
send    -2.0\r
wait    3
send    4.0\r
wait    3
send    32\r
wait    60

shot    phoon
size    25 80
cap     The moon for 7 October 2025, lit from the correct side, with the days
cap     and hours since the full moon and to the last quarter beside it.  With
cap     no argument it draws tonight's, and `-l' sets the height.  One run in
cap     seventeen, by the clock, draws the moon in the letters GREENCHEESE
cap     instead of @.
try     phoon 2025 10 7
run     phoon 2025 10 7
wait    3

shot    globe
size    28 80
cap     globe draws the Earth as it is lit right now, one of thirty ASCII
cap     views spread across the day, so the globe turns from one run to the
cap     next as the hours pass.  It takes no options.
try     globe
run     globe
wait    3

shot    spew
size    24 80
cap     Four headlines; a rules file of your own makes other kinds of
cap     nonsense.
try     spew 4
run     spew 4
wait    2

shot    chef
size    24 80
cap     chef rewrites English into the Swedish Chef's mock accent a letter
cap     and syllable at a time -- the becomes zee, w becomes v, o becomes
cap     oo -- and barks "Bork Bork Bork!" at the end of every sentence.
cap     Pipe any text through it.
try     echo "The Swedish chef boils the water and stirs the soup." | chef
run     echo "The Swedish chef boils the water and stirs the soup." | chef
wait    2

shot    fudd
size    24 80
cap     His own line, run through it.
try     echo "Be very very quiet, I am hunting rabbits." | fudd
run     echo "Be very very quiet, I am hunting rabbits." | fudd
wait    2

shot    pig
size    24 80
cap     One sentence through it.
try     echo "Pig latin was a language popular in the forties." | pig
run     echo "Pig latin was a language popular in the forties." | pig
wait    2

shot    repunsel
size    24 80
cap     repunsel swaps English words for puns on plants and gardens, in
cap     capitals: and becomes ANT, would WOOD, not KNOT.
try     echo "And I would root for you, but not over the tree." | repunsel
run     echo "And I would root for you, but not over the tree." | repunsel
wait    2

shot    drawl
size    24 80
cap     drawl gives text a broad Texan accent, dropping g's and stretching
cap     vowels -- going to becomes gunna, to becomes tuh, the becomes thuh.
cap     Pipe any text through it.
try     echo "I am going to the store to buy some food." | drawl
run     echo "I am going to the store to buy some food." | drawl
wait    2

shot    biff
size    24 80
cap     biff writes like B1FF, the Usenet caricature of an over-excited
cap     newcomer: all capitals, zeros for o's, and exclamation marks with
cap     a 1 among them.  Pipe any text through it.
try     echo "Please mend the computer program, thanks, you are cool" | biff
run     echo "Please mend the computer program, thanks, you are cool" | biff
wait    2

shot    discord
size    24 80
cap     discord learns which word follows which in the text it is given,
cap     then writes new paragraphs by following those chains at random.
cap     Here it has read the disk's own note on ksh.
try     discord < README-KSH | head -n 20
run     builtin cd /dd/DOC
run     clear
run     discord < README-KSH | head -n 20
wait    4

shot    ken
size    24 80
cap     ken turns English into Cockney, with rhyming slang for the
cap     computer words.  Pipe any text through it.
try     echo "What are you doing with my computer and your mother" | ken
run     echo "What are you doing with my computer and your mother" | ken
wait    2

shot    ogrify
size    24 80
cap     Each word stands a thirty-in-a-hundred chance of being swapped for one
cap     from ogre.words, the list it came with -- here `fox' became `force' --
cap     and the text is folded to lower case and wrapped at 40 columns.  It is
cap     run in DOC/ogrify, where the list is.
try     echo "The quick brown fox jumps over a dog" | ogrify ogre.words -30
run     builtin cd /dd/DOC/ogrify
run     clear
wait    1
run     echo "The quick brown fox jumps over a dog" | ogrify ogre.words -30
wait    2

shot    sifi
size    24 80
cap     sifi writes the plot of a science-fiction film: who comes to
cap     Earth, what they want, and how it ends.  The number is how many
cap     chapters.
try     sifi -2
run     sifi -2
wait    2

shot    taxlaw
size    24 80
cap     taxlaw writes imitation tax regulations, section and paragraph
cap     references included.  The number is how many sentences.
try     taxlaw 1
run     taxlaw 1
wait    2

shot    lame
size    24 80
cap     lame rewrites its arguments into IRC "leet-speak" -- o becomes 0 or
cap     (), you becomes U, and becomes &, i becomes 1, e becomes 3 -- with a
cap     little randomness, the way a lamer types.  Give it a sentence.
try     lame you are so cool and elite
run     lame you are so cool and elite
wait    2

shot    westley
size    24 80
cap     Pulls the petals off a daisy, one at a time.  It won Best Layout in
cap     the 1990 contest: its source is written to be read as a letter, and
cap     the judges read the opening as "charlie, doubletime me, OXFACE!".
try     westley 7
run     clear
wait    1
run     westley 7
wait    3


shot    theorem
size    24 80
cap     The 1990 contest's Best of Show: it solves y'=f(x,y) by Runge-Kutta,
cap     a step at a time.  Given y'=y starting at 1, the value at x=1 is e.
cap     The same source is also a reversing filter, and two more programs.
try     theorem y 0 1 0.1 1
run     clear
wait    1
run     theorem y 0 1 0.1 1
wait    4

shot    dds
size    24 80
cap     A BASIC interpreter in 1536 characters of C, a winner of the
cap     International Obfuscated C Code Contest.  It prompts `Ok'; here a
cap     five-line program, then LIST, RUN and BYE, come from a file, and the
cap     program is listed back and run.  Type in capitals.
try     dds < tmp/dds.in
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp > /nil 2>/nil; printf '10 PRINT "HELLO OS-9"\r20 FOR I = 1 TO 3\r30 PRINT I\r40 NEXT I\r50 END\rLIST\rRUN\rBYE\r' > /dd/tmp/dds.in
wait    2
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     dds < tmp/dds.in
wait    4


shot    trigraph
size    24 80
cap     A program that prints its own source, spelled with ANSI C trigraphs:
cap     ??( and ??) for brackets, ??< and ??> for braces, ??= for #, ??/ for \.
try     trigraph | head -n 22
os9     trigraph ! head -n 22
run     clear
wait    1
run     trigraph | head -n 22
wait    3

shot    telewords
size    24 80
cap     The first eighteen of the 27 ways to spell 468 on a telephone keypad,
cap     got and hot among them.
try     telewords 468 | head -n 18
os9     telewords 468 ! head -n 18
run     clear
wait    1
run     telewords 468 | head -n 18
wait    2

shot    telenum
size    24 80
cap     The telephone number each word dials, one to a line.
try     telenum hello world goodbye
run     clear
wait    1
run     telenum hello world goodbye
wait    2

shot    psychic
size    24 80
cap     Two psychic messages, each strung together at random from stock
cap     phrases, so no two runs read alike.
try     psychic 2
run     clear
wait    1
run     psychic 2
wait    2

shot    lotto
size    34 80
cap     Asked whether to hear a testimonial, whether you believe and whether
cap     you really believe -- y to all three -- it draws six numbers.
try     lotto -a 1
run     clear
wait    1
run     lotto -a 1
wait    3
send    y\r
wait    3
send    y\r
wait    3
send    y\r
wait    12

shot    roll
cap     Three rolls: three six-sided dice, six sets of the best three of four
cap     dice, and two ten-sided dice twice over.
try     roll 3d6; roll 6x3,4d6; roll 2@2d10
run     clear
wait    1
run     roll 3d6
wait    2
run     roll 6x3,4d6
wait    2
run     roll 2@2d10
wait    3

shot    smiley
cap     Two faces explained -- every meaning the list holds for each -- and the
cap     version line, which counts the list as it was built.
try     smiley "8-)" "*<|:-)"; smiley -V
run     clear
wait    1
run     smiley "8-)" "*<|:-)"
wait    3
run     smiley -V
wait    3
snap
